{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1502951743,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUyNTA4NDI4NzI=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1723","origin":"github"},"title":"`datalad diff` cannot detect untracked files","message":"For a normal use of `diff` this is a non-issue. However, using `diff` for modification detection within `annotate-paths` requires that too. On one hand, we want `annotate-path` to `diff` recorded history (e.g. in the context of deciding if a `publish` is needed), on the other hand we want to discover dirty bits in a dataset (e.g. in the context of #1720). As `annotate-paths` is unaware of the context it should be able to do both.\n\nI propose to: whenever no commit range is given (e.g. not HEAD~2..HEAD, but just HEAD), `datalad diff` also reports any untracked content, and does so via an additional call to `git --work-tree=. status --untracked=all --porcelain --ignore-submodules=all`. This should work in direct mode too.\n\nTogether with this change, `diff` must also grow some basic unit tests.","files":null}]}